Summary:
The implementation of Graph,
DFS (Depth First Search), 
BFS (Breadth First Search)
It was successfully performed using Python.
A graph was represented using an adjacency list, which efficiently stores the connections between vertices.
DFS explores the graph deeply by using recursion, while BFS explores vertices level by level using a queue.
Both traversal techniques visit vertices and edges systematically and have a time complexity of O(V + E).
Conclusion:
Graph traversal is an important concept in data structures and algorithms.
DFS is useful when exploring paths deeply, whereas BFS is useful for level-by-level exploration and finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph.
Through this implementation, the working principles of graph representation, DFS, and BFS were understood and demonstrated successfully in Python.
